# 48 (found while verifying the named-constraint-alias idiom for
# docs/reference-language.md, then re-probed after the first assessment
# proved too narrow).
#
# FIXED 2026-08-28, and REGRADED from minor to critical on the way.
#
# Filed as a message difference: both ports refused a violating value
# with the same code and the same path, and named different residuals.
# Re-probing found the message was the symptom of a CANON SOUNDNESS
# defect in BOTH ports. The canon of the composition below dropped
# `max(15)` -- the atom added at the point of use -- so `aontu -c`
# printed, and `aontu hash` pinned, a document admitting 20, which this
# document rejects. Canon did not round-trip meaning, and the two ports
# hashed one document differently, which is a parity break at the trust
# layer. `subsume`, `breaking` and `diff` all read that canon.
#
#   $ aontu -c composed-alias-atom-dropped.aon
#   {"a":min(0)&max(15),...}   # now, both ports
#   {"a":min(0),...}           # before, BOTH ports -- max(15) gone
#
# ENFORCEMENT WAS NEVER AFFECTED, which is how it survived: every
# accepting case agreed byte-for-byte across both ports and every
# violating case refused in both, before the fix as after. Only the
# value written down for the composition was wrong.
#
# The cause. When the referent is completed by a LATER statement (`b`
# here is written twice) the reference resolves a pass late, so the
# locally-conjoined atom arrives as a PEER of an ExpectVal.
# ExpectVal.unify computed the meet, saw it was still not generable,
# and rebuilt the surviving node from the ORIGINAL peg -- keeping the
# meet only in `peer`, which canon does not render. Every later copy
# (the bag's expect re-wrap, Val.clone) rebuilt from `peg` too, so the
# atom was dropped again at each one. The meet is now the new peg: an
# expectation that has met a peer without being freed by it stands for
# `peg & peer` from then on. That needs no new field and no carrying,
# because every copy site already preserves `peg`.
#
# Pinned by test/spec/constraint-alias.tsv (alias-violated-* and
# alias-*-canon). Reverting the fix separates the ports exactly as the
# diagnosis predicts: Go fails seven rows, TypeScript two -- the two
# being the plain-layered pair, the half both ports got wrong.
#
# expected: canon of $.a is min(0)&max(15) in both ports
# actual:   canon of $.a is min(0)&max(15) (was min(0) in both)
# run: node ../../ts/bin/aontu.js -c composed-alias-atom-dropped.aon
b: {z: 1}
b: {u8: min(0)}
a: $.b.u8 & max(15)
